Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Lap Forty-Two Needs Another Breath

Story & Context: During an early lap-count rehearsal, Lin chooses forty-two as the formal number and attempts to sustain a repeating bawu figure across forty-two short rhythmic cycles. By the twenty-ninth cycle, her natural breathing pattern begins shifting the phrase away from Marcus’s drum grid. Instead of correcting the drift, the band makes the inhalations structural. Every breath triggers a three-kick substitution and a damped dulcimer answer.

Zhao limits herself to two prepared dyads, each cut off before the next snare. Eli enters only after the sixth audible breath with a low euphonium tone slightly sharp to the electronic bass. He settles into unison only on cycle forty-two. The track’s final restart occurs after Lin deliberately takes a longer breath than expected, forcing the drums to wait rather than fill the entire space.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Chalk number 42 written on the inner line of a steep concrete velodrome, bawu resting across the marking, compact drum machine on a low timing table, felt-damped hammered-dulcimer strings nearby, euphonium bell catching dim overhead light. Spoken fragments limited to “forty-two,” “take the breath,” “do not fill all of it,” and “one more lap.”

Track 2: Felt Strip Across the Fastest Bar

Story & Context: Zhao notices that one dulcimer string course rings too long to survive Marcus’s fastest kick pattern. She places a thin felt strip across the strings, shortening the note into a dry double knock. The altered sound becomes the track’s central hook: every four bars, the dulcimer interrupts the electronic pattern with an acoustic attack that sounds almost sampled but is performed live.

Lin responds with narrow bawu bends that begin on the second half of each dulcimer strike. Marcus gradually increases rhythmic density without changing tempo, moving from sparse kicks to tight ghettotech alternations. Eli avoids sustained notes entirely for the first two minutes, using only short cup-muted puffs beneath selected downbeats.

In the bridge, Zhao removes the felt. The restored ringing sustain suddenly makes the entire room feel slower. The final section replaces the strip and the groove snaps back into its compact form.

